{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>But if he be poorer than your valuation.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'keep him alive by your valuation' (החייהו מערכך) — to say that if one vowed 'my valuation is upon me' and he is obligated to give the fixed valuation stated in the portion according to his years, in order to fulfill his vow and his consecration, and he is poor and does not wish to give, the court enters his house and seizes his property and leaves him what suffices for his livelihood from his own valuation, and takes the rest; and with this he fulfills his consecration even though he did not pay his entire valuation, and even if he later became wealthy he owes nothing more. And this law regarding one who gives a valuation is like a creditor: for if the borrower does not have the amount of the debt, the court's agent who enters his house leaves him what he needs for his livelihood and takes the rest — for we derive by a gezerah shavah: 'makh' here and 'makh' there — it is written here, 'your valuation' (בערכך), 'and if he be poorer than your valuation' (ואם מך הוא מערכך), and it is written regarding a creditor, 'and if your brother becomes poor' (וכי ימוך אחיך) — just as there they arrange payment, so here they arrange payment. But there is this difference between them: for in the case of a creditor the Torah obligated returning the pledge of a daytime garment and a nighttime garment, but did not so obligate in the case of one who gives a valuation, for the court is not obligated to return to him a daytime garment and a nighttime garment. The reason for this matter is explained in the Gemara in Bava Metzia at the end of the chapter HaMekabel, where we say there: Scripture says (Deuteronomy 24:13), 'ושכב בשלמתו וברכך' — 'who needs blessing,' that is, an ordinary person; this excludes consecrated property, which does not need blessing. And there they raise an objection: but is it not written (Deuteronomy 8:10), 'ואכלת ושבעת וברכת' — and from this we learn that consecrated property does need blessing, since it warns 'וברכת'; and they answer: rather, the reason is because it is written (Deuteronomy 24), 'ולך תהיה צדקה' — 'who needs charity,' this excludes consecrated property, which does not need charity, for all charities are His. And you should be roused to understand how they concluded there in the Gemara that consecrated property does not need charity but does need blessing; and this is clear to the discerning." "<b>And he shall stand him before the priest, and the priest shall value him; the priest shall value him. (12) And the priest shall value. According to your valuation, O priest. (14) And the priest shall value it; as the priest values it. (18) And the priest shall reckon for him. (21) To the priest shall his holding be. (23) And the priest shall reckon for him.</b> Ten 'priests' are written in this portion. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said in tractate Megillah, chapter HaKorei et HaMegillah: 'The lands — nine and a priest, and a person like them.' The explanation of this is: one who consecrated land and comes to redeem it needs ten men to appraise it, and the nine shall be Israelites and one of them a priest. And from this they said in the Gemara: ten 'priests' are written in this portion; remove one for its own sake, and the others constitute a limitation after a limitation, and a limitation after a limitation is nothing but an inclusion — that the nine should be Israelites and one of them a priest."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:29:08.102867639Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}